 going out in the world  get back to the baseline level because  it was never zero even before  kovid you could go out and catch the flu  you could catch a stomach bug  you could get run over by a bus etc so  it's about going back to the baseline  we were seeing even in the great economy  that i agree was was in place  uh during the trump administration  before the pandemic hit great in many  ways and yet we still had stagnant wages  and other issues  you still saw 200 something new uh 200  something thousand  new unemployment claims every week so  when you look at that number 385  it's almost back down to the pre  pandemic level  this is further evidence that as  people's unemployment benefits uh  continue  start to and continue to run out and as  more and more aspects of the economy are  reopened  as more and more people's kids go back  to school so that parents who don't have  child care can actually get back to work  we are going to see the issue that the  right  claims is a big issue of people just  hanging out at home  raking in the dough on unemployment it's  going to resolve itself the unemployment  is going to end the enhanced  unemployment ends in september  people state unemployment will  ultimately run out even with the various  extensions that different states provide  and people are going to have to go back  to work now there's going to be a real  question here  about whether we see stronger lobbying  efforts  uh for better wages and you know the the  economy is a complicated thing when we  say  what about a 15 minimum wage as i said  before there are parts of the country  where cost of living is so high that a  15  minimum wage is too low that's just the  reality there are other parts of the  country  where the cost of living is very very  low  and going to a 15 minimum wage  immediately  actually could be a significant shock to  the economy particularly states  that still have the federal minimum wage  as the prevailing minimum wage  and so these are really regionalized  issues much the same way  depending on which state you live in and  depending on what the cost of living is  and depending on what  the wages are that are being paid there  there are jobs that as a result of all  of this are probably going to have to  start paying people more in order to get  people to come back to work  part of that is going to be because of  some large businesses everything from  you know amazon bank of america has made  an announcement a  similar announcement have upped the  minimum wage that they are going to pay  this is going to lead to some businesses  having to pay more in order to attract  people  and that's okay now if you said they're  going to have to pay 100 bucks an hour  that's a straw man red herring that's  not an equilibrium minimum wage it's not  adjusted to cost of living it's not  adjusted to anything that makes sense  it's a straw man from the right  they love to say hey listen people  having more money is better  make the minimum wage 100 bucks an hour  make it a thousand bucks an hour  nobody's calling for that that's a  serious person because that's not an  equilibrium  wage tied to inflation productivity cost  of living or anything else  that being said remember that when  people  make more money that is a cost to the  employer  but then people in the community have  more money  to go and spend at that business imagine  a hardware store for example  this is often an example that's given  hardware store all of a sudden has a  higher wage they have to pay either  because of the minimum wage has gone up  or because  they need to raise wages to attract  workers they go from  eight bucks an hour to 12 bucks an hour  uh oh their payroll costs have just gone  up that's terrible right that'll kill  their business  well hold on a second if the entire  region now sees  a 12 minimum wage instead of eight you  now have  all sorts of people who because they are  earning more money  they're going to go to the hardware  store and say hey you know what i'm  going to get a new vacuum  or i'm going to get what i need to  refinish my floors or i'm going to  finish my basement or whatever the case  may be  and even though the hardware store now  has to pay more out in wages  they have all of these customers that  now have money to go and spend and do  projects so  revenue goes up as well this analysis  only works  within what we would consider an  equilibrium wage  it's really important to understand that  so  that's the latest in terms of  unemployment claims this is going to be  resolved  mostly on its own some businesses are  going to have to start paying more  there's no question about that because  they have been paying  sub market wages for a really long time  uh  and that is going to be a problem for  them for the most part this issue is  going to resolve itself i'm already  seeing it happen  in these numbers let me know your  thoughts you can find me on twitter at d  pacman we've got a really great show for  you today
 

